          "Flexbox is a one-dimensional CSS layout model for arranging items in a row or column along a main axis. Apply display: flex to a container element to turn its direct children into flex items. The flex-direction property sets the main axis direction: row (horizontal, default), row-reverse, column (vertical), or column-reverse. This makes Flexbox ideal for navigation bars, card rows, and vertically centered content.",
          "The justify-content property aligns flex items along the main axis. Values include flex-start (default, packed to start), flex-end (packed to end), center (centered), space-between (equal space between items), space-around (equal space around items), and space-evenly (equal space everywhere). Use align-items to align items along the cross axis (perpendicular to the main axis) with values like stretch, flex-start, flex-end, and center.",
          "Flex items can grow, shrink, and have a base size controlled by three properties. flex-grow determines how much extra space an item absorbs (0 means no growth). flex-shrink determines how items compress when space is tight (1 is default). flex-basis sets the initial size before free space is distributed. The shorthand flex: 1 sets flex-grow: 1, flex-shrink: 1, and flex-basis: 0%, making items share space equally.",
          "The flex-wrap property controls whether items stay on one line or wrap to new lines. nowrap (default) keeps all items on a single line, which may cause overflow. wrap allows items to flow onto additional lines, and wrap-reverse wraps in the opposite direction. Combined with flex-direction, wrap enables responsive multi-row layouts without media queries.",
