          "The event object passed to handlers contains useful properties. event.target refers to the element that triggered the event, while event.currentTarget refers to the element the listener is attached to (they differ during event delegation). event.preventDefault() stops the browser's default action, such as following a link or submitting a form. event.stopPropagation() prevents the event from bubbling to parent elements.",
          "Event propagation has two phases: capturing (from document down to target) and bubbling (from target back up to document). By default, listeners fire during the bubbling phase. Pass true as the third argument to addEventListener to listen during capturing instead. Most code uses bubbling, which is the default behavior.",
          "Event delegation attaches a single listener to a parent element instead of individual listeners on each child. Because events bubble, clicking a child button inside a <ul> fires the ul's listener, where you check event.target to identify which child was clicked. This pattern is efficient for dynamic lists where items are added or removed frequently.",
          "Common event types include click and dblclick for mouse interaction, mouseenter and mouseleave for hover states, keydown and keyup for keyboard input, input and change for form field updates, submit for form submission, load and DOMContentLoaded for page lifecycle, and scroll and resize for viewport changes. Touch events (touchstart, touchmove, touchend) handle mobile interaction.",
