          "Web accessibility (a11y) ensures that websites are usable by people with disabilities, including visual, auditory, motor, and cognitive impairments. An estimated one billion people worldwide have some form of disability, and accessible design also benefits users on slow connections, bright sunlight, or mobile devices. Many countries have legal requirements such as the ADA in the United States and EN 301 549 in the European Union.",
          "Semantic HTML is the foundation of accessibility. Using proper heading hierarchy, landmark elements (<main>, <nav>, <aside>), and native form controls provides built-in accessibility without extra effort. Native <button> elements are keyboard-focusable and announce their role to screen readers automatically, unlike <div onclick> which requires manual ARIA attributes and tabindex to become accessible.",
          "ARIA (Accessible Rich Internet Applications) attributes supplement HTML when native semantics are insufficient. Use aria-label to provide an accessible name, aria-describedby to link descriptive text, aria-hidden=\"true\" to hide decorative elements from screen readers, and role to define an element's purpose. The first rule of ARIA is: do not use ARIA when a native HTML element already provides the needed semantics.",
          "Keyboard accessibility requires that all interactive elements are reachable and operable via Tab, Enter, Space, and arrow keys. Visible focus indicators (outline styles) show which element is currently focused. Skip links at the top of the page let keyboard users bypass repetitive navigation and jump directly to the main content area.",
          "Color contrast ratios ensure text is readable against its background. WCAG 2.1 Level AA requires at least 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text. Never convey information through color alone—pair color changes with icons, text labels, or patterns so users who cannot distinguish colors still understand the message.",
          "Testing accessibility combines automated tools and manual verification. Tools like axe DevTools and Lighthouse catch common issues such as missing alt text and insufficient contrast. Manual testing with keyboard-only navigation and a screen reader (NVDA, VoiceOver) reveals issues that automated tools miss, such as illogical focus order or confusing dynamic content announcements."
