          "Version control systems track changes to files over time, enabling developers to review history, revert mistakes, and collaborate without overwriting each other's work. Git is the dominant version control system in web development, used locally and hosted on platforms like GitHub, GitLab, and Bitbucket. Every project should use version control from its first commit.",
          "A Git repository contains the full history of a project. The working directory holds your current files, the staging area (index) holds changes prepared for the next commit, and the repository stores all committed snapshots. The command git add stages changes, git commit saves a snapshot with a descriptive message, and git status shows what has changed.",
          "Commits should be atomic (one logical change per commit) with clear messages explaining why the change was made. Good messages follow a pattern like \"fix: prevent form submission on empty email field\" rather than vague text like \"updates\" or \"fix bug.\" Conventional prefixes like feat, fix, docs, and refactor help teams scan history quickly.",
          "Branches allow parallel development without affecting the main codebase. Create a branch with git checkout -b feature/contact-form, make commits on it, then merge back into main when the feature is complete. The main (or master) branch typically holds production-ready code, while feature branches isolate work in progress.",
          "Remote repositories on GitHub enable team collaboration. git remote add origin <url> links a local repo to a remote, git push uploads commits, and git pull downloads and merges remote changes. Pull requests (or merge requests) provide a review workflow where teammates examine code changes, run automated tests, and discuss improvements before merging.",
          "Essential Git commands for daily web development include git log (view history), git diff (see unstaged changes), git stash (temporarily save uncommitted work), git revert (undo a commit with a new commit), and git clone (copy a remote repository locally). Understanding these commands prevents data loss and enables confident collaboration on team projects."
