          "Unit tests isolate a function or class, replacing dependencies with test doubles such as mocks, stubs, or fakes. They run in milliseconds and pinpoint failures precisely. Good unit tests follow Arrange-Act-Assert: set up state, invoke behavior, assert outcomes. Tests should be deterministic—no reliance on wall-clock timing, network, or random order without seeding.",
          "Integration tests verify that modules work together: database repositories against a real or containerized database, HTTP handlers against an in-memory server, message consumers with a test broker. They catch wiring mistakes unit tests miss but cost more to run and debug. Contract tests between services ensure producers and consumers agree on API shapes.",
